  "query_preview": "/Saturday through Early Next Week/...\n\nAs we transition into the weekend, the broader synoptic pattern\nsupports a return to drier conditions by Saturday. Subsidence \nwill overspread the region in the wake of the departing mid-week\ntrough, allowing a prominent, anomalous upper-level heat ridge \ncurrently anchored to our west to expand eastward into the Mid- \nMississippi River Valley.\n\nWith rising mid-level heights, uncomfortable summertime heat is\npoised to make a return. High temperatures are forecast to push\nback into the low 90s. Factoring in the pooling of remnant \nboundary layer moisture, heat index values will likely creep \nback toward the 100-degree mark by Sunday and early next week.\n\nHowever, a major caveat accompanies this heat forecast. Our \nposition on the northeast periphery of the expanding upper ridge\nwill place central Illinois in a classic ring of fire regime. \nNorthwest flow aloft will leave the door wide open for subtle, \ndifficult-to-time mid-level shortwaves to crest the ridge and \ndive southeastward into the region. This pattern is notorious \nfor breeding nocturnal and early-day Mesoscale Convective \nSystems (MCSs). Should these thunderstorm complexes materialize,\nthe associated precipitation and convective cloud debris would \neasily thwart the intense heat forecast. At this range, ensemble\nand multi-model guidance depict considerably more noise than \nsignal regarding these convective chances, meaning temperature \ntrends early next week will be heavily--and frustratingly-- \ndependent on mesoscale convective evolution.",
